  "result": "At dawn, the city belongs to small sounds. A delivery truck sighs beside the curb. Somewhere above, a window slides open, and a spoon circles inside a mug. Pigeons negotiate the roofline with solemn nods, as if deciding which clouds may pass.\n\nI like this hour because nothing has fully committed to being itself. The bakery is only a warm rectangle of light. The avenue, usually impatient, rests under traffic signals changing colors for no one. Even the river looks undecided, carrying both the gray of night and the first copper hint of morning.\n\nA cyclist appears, wrapped in a red scarf. He glides through the empty intersection and vanishes, leaving the scene subtly altered. That is the strange power of passing figures: they enter our attention for seconds, then remain in memory without names.\n\nSoon, doors will open. Espresso machines will hiss. Phones will vibrate across desks, and elevators will swallow crowds floor by floor. Plans, apologies, invoices, jokes, and weather reports will gather speed. The day will become useful, measurable, and loud.\n\nBut before that happens, there is this brief republic of quiet, shared by bakers, cleaners, insomniacs, nurses heading home, and anyone who has wandered outside early enough to see the world rehearsing.\n\nPerhaps peace is not the absence of motion, but motion without demand: steam rising, wheels turning, water moving beneath bridges. For a little while, everything continues without asking to be admired. Then sunlight reaches the windows, and the ordinary miracle begins again, unnoticed, everywhere.",
